---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CallLink from "../components/CallLink.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Small water leak cleanup you can do yourself"
  description="How to dry a small appliance leak or drip in a Georgia home, and when to stop and call a restoration company."
>
  <article class="hero wrap">
    <h1>Small leaks you can handle yourself</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A dripping fridge line or a wet spot under the sink is not the same job
      as a burst pipe at 2 a.m. If the water is already off and the mess is
      small, you can often dry it. If you are not sure, call or send the form.
    </p>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="card">
        <h2>Usually OK to dry yourself</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>The leak has stopped or you shut the valve.</li>
          <li>You can see all the water — one room, a few square feet.</li>
          <li>No sewage, no sagging ceiling, no wet outlets.</li>
          <li>It has been wet for a few hours, not days.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h2>Stop and get a crew</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Sewage or a toilet backup.</li>
          <li>Ceiling bulge, water in walls, or a wet breaker panel.</li>
          <li>Water you cannot shut off.</li>
          <li>Carpet pad, hardwood, or more than one room is soaked.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Do-it-yourself steps</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Shut the local valve (under the sink, behind the toilet, or behind the fridge). If you cannot find it, use the house main.</li>
      <li>If cords and outlets are dry, unplug nearby appliances. If they are wet, leave them and call.</li>
      <li>Photograph the room and the source before you move much. Insurance still matters on a “small” job that grows.</li>
      <li>Pull standing water with towels or a wet vac. Lift furniture onto blocks or dry boards.</li>
      <li>Get wet rugs and the pad out if you can. Leave wet drywall and ceilings alone — do not cut holes unless you know what is in the wall.</li>
      <li>Run a fan and a dehumidifier. Open windows only if the air outside is drier than the room.</li>
      <li>Check again the next morning. If it still smells, feels damp, or has spread, that is a restoration job.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2>If it is more than a small leak</h2>
    <p>
      Send the form or call. A restoration company can talk insurance with
      you. You decide whether to hire them.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="/#request">Send a request</a>
      <CallLink class="btn secondary" />
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/faq">FAQ</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
